<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    Doctrine mapping for the `Identity` context's THIRD aggregate root.

    It needs no config change, and that was verified rather than assumed: the `Identity` block in
    `config/packages/doctrine.yaml` maps this whole directory (`type: xml`, `dir:
    .../Persistence/Doctrine/mapping`, `prefix: App\Domain\Identity\Entity`), so dropping a file in
    here is all it takes for `doctrine:mapping:info` to list the entity. One mapping block per
    bounded context (ADR-0007 decision 3) paying off for the second time. What is *not* free is the
    two custom DBAL type names below — a type is global to the connection and has to be registered
    under `doctrine.dbal.types` by hand.

    Everything ADR-0007 established for `User.orm.xml` applies unchanged — XML so that no
    `use Doctrine\...` lands in `Domain/`, explicit table and column names so that renaming a PHP
    property can never silently rename a production column, `<generator strategy="NONE"/>` because
    ids are minted by the repository, `datetimetz_immutable` so a stored instant is absolute.

    This file is a near-twin of `EmailVerificationRequest.orm.xml`, which sits beside it, and the
    resemblance is load-bearing rather than lazy: the two aggregates share a *storage shape* while
    sharing almost no behaviour (see the aggregate's docblock for the four inversions). A mapping is
    exactly the layer where sameness of shape is the only thing that matters, so this is the one
    place the duplication costs nothing at all. Two columns are new — `invalidated_at`, which no
    verification request has, is inversion #2 in physical form.

    WHY `user_id` IS A PLAIN TYPED COLUMN AND NOT A `<many-to-one>` ASSOCIATION (AC-40).

    The reflex — and what every Doctrine tutorial writes, and what
    `symfonycasts/reset-password-bundle`'s own maker generates — is
    `<many-to-one field="user" target-entity="User"/>`. It is wrong here, and for a modelling reason
    rather than a performance one. `PasswordResetRequest` holds a `UserId`, not a `User`: aggregates
    reference each other **by identity**, so that no caller can reach across an aggregate boundary
    and mutate a second root through the first. An association would put a real `User` object (or a
    lazy proxy of one) on this aggregate and hand exactly that power to anyone holding a request. It
    would also let Doctrine's cascade settings quietly decide the transactional semantics that
    ADR-0011 argued out explicitly — and here that argument is sharper than it was in slice 2,
    because redemption's two saves happen in a **deliberately inverted order** (request first, user
    second) chosen so that a crash leaves a burnt token and an unchanged password rather than the
    reverse. A cascade would take that choice away from the handler that reasoned about it.

    The mapping therefore does what the Domain already does — stores the identity, typed. Reusing
    the existing `identity_user_id` DBAL type (rather than a bare `guid`, and rather than minting a
    fourth type) means the column still round-trips a real `UserId` value object, so nothing is lost
    except the association.

    WHY THERE IS NO FOREIGN KEY TO `identity_user` (ADR-0009 decision 4).

    Follows from the same idea, and is worth stating in the mapping because a future reader will
    otherwise "fix" it in a migration. Referential integrity *between aggregates* is the
    application's job, not the schema's. And a hand-added FK that Doctrine's mapping does not know
    about is permanent noise: every subsequent `make migration.make` would diff it as an unwanted
    constraint and offer to drop it, so it would be deleted-and-re-added forever by whoever ran the
    generator last. The accepted cost is that orphan rows become possible if a user is ever
    hard-deleted — nothing deletes users today, the `identity-challenge-pruning` job owed later (now
    owed by *two* tables) will sweep them, and the GDPR-erasure conversation before public launch
    must not forget this table either.
-->
<doctrine-mapping xmlns="http://doctrine-project.org/schemas/orm/doctrine-mapping"
                  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                  xsi:schemaLocation="http://doctrine-project.org/schemas/orm/doctrine-mapping
                                      https://www.doctrine-project.org/schemas/orm/doctrine-mapping.xsd">

    <entity name="App\Domain\Identity\Entity\PasswordResetRequest"
            table="identity_password_reset_request">

        <!--
            One composite index serving two queries, which is why no second index is defined.

            Column order is the whole design. `countIssuedForUserSince()` (the anti-abuse rule I-21)
            matches `user_id` for equality and `issued_at` as a range, and a B-tree can use a range
            predicate only on the first column after all the equality ones — so `(user_id,
            issued_at)` is an index scan over a narrow slice while `(issued_at, user_id)` would scan
            across every user's rows in the window. `findOutstandingForUser()` then rides the
            **leading column only**, which is enough: that query filters further on
            `redeemed_at IS NULL AND invalidated_at IS NULL`, and a partial index on
            `(user_id) WHERE redeemed_at IS NULL AND invalidated_at IS NULL` was considered and
            rejected because the per-user row count is bounded at three per rolling hour and the
            pruning job will keep it small — the composite is already selective enough to make the
            extra index pure write amplification.

            Named here rather than left to Doctrine's `IDX_<hash>` because the migration, the
            adapter's comments and the AC-43 `EXPLAIN` assertions all refer to it by name, and an
            assertion cannot refer to a name nobody chose.
        -->
        <!--
            The second index arrives with `identity-challenge-pruning`, and the sentence just above —
            "which is why no second index is defined" — is the one it contradicts, so the reason is
            here rather than left to be re-derived. The partial index rejected above was declined
            because the composite was already selective enough for a *lookup*; this one serves a
            different access pattern entirely. The sweep asks for the oldest overdue rows in
            `expires_at` order, per batch, up to fifty times per table per run — a leading-column
            range scan that `(user_id, issued_at)` cannot serve at all. The justification is therefore
            the **batching**, not the table's size: fifty sequential scans per run without it, fifty
            range scans down the left edge of a B-tree with it.

            DECLARED IN THIS FILE AS WELL AS IN THE MIGRATION, IN THE SAME COMMIT. An index the
            database has and the mapping does not is diff noise in perpetuity — every later
            `make migration.make` offers to drop it. That is ADR-0009 decision 4's argument against a
            hand-added foreign key, running in the positive direction here: the cure for a generator
            that keeps arguing with a schema object is to tell the mapping the object exists.
        -->
        <indexes>
            <index name="idx_identity_password_reset_request_user_issued" columns="user_id,issued_at"/>
            <index name="idx_identity_password_reset_request_expires_at" columns="expires_at"/>
        </indexes>

        <!--
            The unique index on the digest does two jobs at once, which is why it is a unique
            constraint and not a plain index.

            It serves the only hot query on this table — `findByTokenHash()`, one lookup per click
            on a reset link — and it makes a duplicate digest a database-level impossibility rather
            than a hope. Note what the adapter deliberately does *not* do with it: unlike
            `uniq_identity_user_email`, whose violation `DoctrineUserRepository::save()` translates
            into `EmailAlreadyRegistered`, a violation here is not a business event. It would mean
            two independent draws of 256 CSPRNG bits collided, and the honest answer to that is a
            500 and an alert. See `PasswordResetRequestRepository::save()`'s docblock for the full
            argument.
        -->
        <unique-constraints>
            <unique-constraint name="uniq_identity_password_reset_request_token_hash" columns="token_hash"/>
        </unique-constraints>

        <!--
            `strategy="NONE"` — assigned by the application, minted by
            `PasswordResetRequestRepository::nextIdentity()` before `issue()` is called. That is what
            lets the aggregate record `PasswordResetRequested` carrying its own id without waiting
            for a post-flush sequence value, and what lets a request be built and thrown away on a
            rate-limit refusal having never met the database.
        -->
        <id name="id" type="identity_password_reset_request_id" column="id">
            <generator strategy="NONE"/>
        </id>

        <!-- Reference by identity, not by association — see the header. -->
        <field name="userId" column="user_id" type="identity_user_id" nullable="false"/>

        <!-- Length mirrors `HashedResetToken::MAX_LENGTH`; the VO owns the rule, the column
             enforces it. Deliberately not narrowed to SHA-256's 64 characters: the digest algorithm
             is `RandomResetTokenGenerator`'s choice, and a column that encoded it would have to be
             migrated the day that choice changes. -->
        <field name="tokenHash" column="token_hash" type="identity_reset_token_hash" length="255" nullable="false"/>

        <!--
            `datetimetz_immutable`, as everywhere: Postgres `TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE` stores an
            absolute instant, so a value written by the UTC `Clock` reads back as the same moment
            whatever timezone the writing container had. The type formats through `'Y-m-d H:i:sO'`
            and therefore discards microseconds before column precision is ever consulted — which is
            exactly why `Clock` mandates whole seconds (ADR-0007 decision 8), why `isExpiredAt()`
            compares with a strict `>` (at second granularity a `>=` would shorten every link by up
            to a second), and why the staleness guard I-23 compares with a strict `<` so that a
            same-second tie lets the legitimate user through.

            Both are NOT NULL because `issue()` derives them together and neither can be absent from
            a request that exists.
        -->
        <field name="issuedAt" column="issued_at" type="datetimetz_immutable" nullable="false"/>
        <field name="expiresAt" column="expires_at" type="datetimetz_immutable" nullable="false"/>

        <!--
            TWO nullable instants rather than one `closed_at` plus a reason enum, and rather than a
            pair of booleans. `NULL` means "this has not happened"; any value means "it happened, and
            here is when".

            `redeemed_at` is the single-use property (invariant I-16) in physical form.
            `invalidated_at` is inversion #2 — the column `EmailVerificationRequest` has no need for,
            because issuing a new verification link leaves the old ones alive while issuing a new
            *reset* link must kill them (each live reset token is an independent, repeatable chance
            to set a password).

            Keeping them apart is what lets `findOutstandingForUser()` be a two-column `IS NULL`
            filter on **structure** rather than a judgement, lets "how many resets completed?" and
            "how many were superseded?" be answered separately, and lets the mutual exclusion I-17
            live as two guards in two aggregate methods instead of a state machine spread across a
            column and a discriminator.
        -->
        <field name="redeemedAt" column="redeemed_at" type="datetimetz_immutable" nullable="true"/>
        <field name="invalidatedAt" column="invalidated_at" type="datetimetz_immutable" nullable="true"/>

    </entity>

</doctrine-mapping>
